  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "short_to_ground",
      "components": ["U5"],
      "pins": ["U5.8", "U5.9", "U5.1"],
      "nets": ["VBUS", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the Power sheet the TPS2121RUXR (U5) symbol's right-hand column lists only three pins: OUT on pin 1 (to the VBUS net / TP1), ST on pin 8 (wired to GND), and GND on pin 12. Pin 9 does not appear on the symbol at all, and pin 8 is not connected to OUT/VBUS.",
      "expected": "The specification states for U5: 'OUT (pins 1/1_1/8/8_1) ... onto the VBUS net' and 'ST(pin9)/GND(pin12) go to GND'. That is, physical pad 8 must be an OUT pin (tied to VBUS) and pad 9 must be the ST pin (tied to GND).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet, U5 (TPS2121RUXR) right column: 'OUT 1' to VBUS arrow/TP1, 'ST 8' to GND, 'GND 12' to GND; left column 7,6,5,2,4,3,11,10; no pin 9 drawn"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "a common OUT (pins 1/1_1/8/8_1) feeding the board's unregulated VBUS rail ... ST(pin9)/GND(pin12) go to GND"}
      ],
      "impact": "On the footprint, pad 8 of the TPS2121RUXR is an OUT pin (internally bonded to pad 1). Wiring symbol pin 8 to the ST/GND net therefore shorts the mux output (VBUS) to ground, so the buck converter U8 never receives input and the 3.3 V rail (and the whole board) stays dead; the real status pin (pad 9) is left floating.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Most of the build matches the specification (SPI/CS GPIO mapping, heater-switch wiring, power-mux input/output nets, buck feedback, TVS/diode placements, and all readable passive values), but the U5 TPS2121RUXR symbol's pin numbering deviates from the intended design: ST is shown on pin 8 (to GND) with pin 9 absent, whereas the spec requires OUT on pin 8 and ST on pin 9, which would short the mux output to ground on the assembled board."
}

